{"id":"h291","run":2,"role":"calibration","category":"science-chemistry","question":"A line in the Lyman series of hydrogen has a wavelength of $1.03 \\times 10^{-7} \\mathrm{~m}$. Find the original energy level of the electron.\nIs the correct answer \"3\"?","truth":"yes","model_answer":"yes","correct":true,"source":"TIGER-Lab/MMLU-Pro, test split","claims":[{"text":"Lyman series lines are transitions that end at n = 1.","confidence":0.98,"lean":0.0,"theta":1.5394,"phi":0.2827},{"text":"The Rydberg formula 1/λ = R(1 − 1/n_i²) with R ≈ 1.097e7 m⁻¹ gives the Lyman wavelengths.","confidence":0.97,"lean":0.0,"theta":1.5237,"phi":0.5326},{"text":"n_i = 2 (Lyman-α) gives about 121.6 nm, which does not match.","confidence":0.95,"lean":-0.5,"theta":1.4923,"phi":2.9321},{"text":"n_i = 3 (Lyman-β) gives about 102.6 nm, which matches 1.03e-7 m.","confidence":0.97,"lean":1,"theta":1.5237,"phi":0.0368},{"text":"n_i = 4 gives about 97.2 nm, which does not match.","confidence":0.95,"lean":-0.3,"theta":1.4923,"phi":2.9167},{"text":"Higher levels give shorter wavelengths converging toward about 91 nm, so no other level fits.","confidence":0.93,"lean":0.7,"theta":1.4608,"phi":0.1497},{"text":"The three significant figures of the given value could allow a small mismatch pointing to another level.","confidence":0.1,"lean":-0.4,"theta":0.1571,"phi":-3.1019},{"text":"The gaps between neighbouring lines are large enough that 1.03e-7 m clearly identifies n = 3.","confidence":0.9,"lean":0.8,"theta":1.4137,"phi":0.018},{"text":"The electron falls from n = 3 to n = 1, so the original energy level is 3.","confidence":0.97,"lean":1,"theta":1.5237,"phi":-0.0006}],"relations":[{"i":0,"j":1,"J":0.4,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":2,"J":0.4,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":3,"J":0.6,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":4,"J":0.3,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":5,"J":0.3,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":7,"J":0.2,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":8,"J":0.7,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":2,"J"